  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a bimetal strip?",
      "a": "It is two thin strips of different metals, often brass and steel, bonded together. When it warms, one metal expands more than the other, so the strip bends; when it cools, it straightens again.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a bimetal strip bend?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the strip switch the heater off?",
      "a": "The strip is part of the circuit. Cool, it touches the contact and current flows; as it warms, it bends away from the contact, opening the circuit and turning the heater off.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a thermostat cut the power?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does turning the thermostat knob do?",
      "a": "The knob moves the contact, changing how far the strip must bend before it lets go. A setting that needs more bending means it has to get warmer before the heater switches off.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you set a bimetal thermostat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the heater keep clicking on and off?",
      "a": "Once the heater is off, the strip slowly cools and straightens, touches the contact again and switches the heater back on. This loop keeps the temperature close to the setting.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a thermostat click?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do modern thermostats still use bimetal strips?",
      "a": "Many simple devices still do, like irons, toasters and some heaters. Digital room thermostats usually use an electronic temperature sensor and a chip instead, but the on-off idea is the same.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a digital thermostat?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Brass expands about one and a half times as much as steel for the same rise in temperature.",
    "Bimetal strips are also used in irons, toasters and many circuit breakers."
  ],
